        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.0",
          "heading": "why_in_hospital",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "Your child came in because she had been drinking a lot and passing a lot of urine for 2 weeks. She had also lost weight. Then she became unwell with vomiting, tummy pain and drowsiness.\n\nThe team found that your child has type 1 diabetes. She also had a serious complication called diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). This is when the body builds up harmful acids in the blood."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "The team ran blood tests when your child arrived. These showed high blood sugar, high ketones and acid in the blood. By day 2 (18/05), the blood tests were improving.\n\nTo treat the DKA, the team:\n- gave fluids through a drip\n- gave insulin through a drip\n- replaced potassium\n- checked her closely every hour\n\nThe intensive care team (PICU) was kept informed. Your child was cared for in the high-dependency area of the ward.\n\nOnce the acid in the blood settled, your child moved to insulin injections. She now has a basal-bolus insulin regimen. This means she has a background insulin and a mealtime insulin.\n\nA diabetes nurse and a dietician worked with your family. The team taught you and your child about:\n- counting carbohydrates\n- managing low blood sugar (hypos)\n- what to do when your child is unwell (sick-day rules)\n\nBefore going home, you and your child felt confident with giving injections and checking blood sugar."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
